REALROM sample.warn c9e1 [NesBankingAnalyzer] Bank state becomes unknown here: call to bank-switch helper FUN_c9bc, whose latched value is re-derived inside the helper under this call site's registers (no caller argument is consulted), and that derivation did not resolve here
REALROM sample.note c4da [NesBankingAnalyzer] Bank value is RESTORED here, not resolved: this call re-commits the bank READ BACK at c43c from bank-identifying ROM byte 8000 -- a byte whose value is the bank it is read from, so the byte read is the bank live at the read, carried on the stack across control flow c43f..c4d9 -- the save/restore idiom. The bank after this call is whatever 8000 held at that read, not a fresh value this analyzer failed to pin down. That read sits at the ENTRY of NMI (nothing switches between its entry and the read), so it is the bank live on entry -- and that entry is an INTERRUPT entry, whose bank is the interrupted code's: arbitrary mainline context, no number on any path. The bank is unchanged from handler entry. A property of the game, not a gap in analysis.
REALROM sample.note c650 [CopyLoopAnalyzer] copy-shaped loop RAM:0133 -> RAM:8000 (8 bytes) recognized but NOT materialized: nothing jumps into the destination and the payload does not decode as a subroutine, so there is no evidence it holds code. If it does, materialize it with the Run From Elsewhere Transfer script or a descriptor copied_from hint.
REALROM sample.note c65c [CopyLoopAnalyzer] copy-shaped loop RAM:013c -> RAM:8000 (8 bytes) recognized but NOT materialized: nothing jumps into the destination and the payload does not decode as a subroutine, so there is no evidence it holds code. If it does, materialize it with the Run From Elsewhere Transfer script or a descriptor copied_from hint.
REALROM sample.note c66e [CopyLoopAnalyzer] copy-shaped loop RAM:0145 -> RAM:8000 (8 bytes) recognized but NOT materialized: nothing jumps into the destination and the payload does not decode as a subroutine, so there is no evidence it holds code. If it does, materialize it with the Run From Elsewhere Transfer script or a descriptor copied_from hint.
REALROM sample.note c9be [NesBankingAnalyzer] Bank value is this helper's ARGUMENT: the backward scan reached the helper's entry, where the register holds whatever the caller supplied, so the bank cannot be known from inside the helper body. Each CALL SITE is resolved separately; see the call sites' own annotations.
REALROM sample.note c9ee [NesBankingAnalyzer] Bank value is RESTORED here, not resolved: this call re-commits the bank READ BACK at c9dc from write-through bank shadow 0049 -- a RAM cell the switch code keeps in step with every bank it commits, so the byte read is the last bank committed through it, carried on the stack across the call at c9e1 -- the save/restore idiom. The bank after this call is whatever 0049 held at that read, not a fresh value this analyzer failed to pin down. That read sits at the ENTRY of FUN_c9c2 (nothing switches between its entry and the read), so it is the shadow as the caller left it: each of its call sites' own bank, which those sites already track -- the call passes it through unchanged. A property of the game, not a gap in analysis.
